{
  "hypothesis": {
    "id": "coverage-guided-fault-placement",
    "parent": null,
    "kind": "add",
    "title": "Choose which node crashes by least-visited fault-context coverage, using a self-contained arm-local visit table",
    "description": "Opt-in coverage signal driving FAULT PLACEMENT, not queue selection, so it does not route through the timeline/steer channel that is inert in the evaluated config (novelty_enabled=false, all steer_terms zero, preferenceExpressed==0 over 956M steps). At the crash-injection site in spur-core/src/simulator/, once the injector has decided a crash occurs this step, replace the uniform pick among eligible nodes with argmin over a small arm-local visit table keyed by a protocol-agnostic fault-context tag built only from fields already carried per node: (a) incarnation count clamped 0/1/2+, (b) whether the node has >=1 sent-but-undelivered message, (c) number of currently-down peers clamped 0/1/2+. Ties consume the same RNG draw as the uniform path so rng_stream_isolation and replay determinism hold. Gate: faults.coverage_guided_placement (bool, default false = today's uniform pick). Table lives in the arm's feedback state, never shared across arms. Counters in util_stats.rs: fault_coverage.{decisions, candidates_ge2, diverged_from_uniform, distinct_tags, max_tag_visits}.",
    "category": "feedback",
    "buildsOn": [
      "crash/recover fault injection",
      "campaign per-arm feedback state",
      "util_stats counters"
    ],
    "expectedGain": 5,
    "expectedCost": 3,
    "rationale": "Red team first: depth is budget-invariant to two significant figures across a 4x iteration range, and every coverage/novelty attempt to date (timer-vs-delivery axis, +2.6x distinct keys, novelty authority sweep, timeline-key granularity) moved no rung; pushing runs into the 2+-recovery stratum may cost throughput exactly where completion is only 6.11%, so the per-second factor can fall as fast as the per-run factor rises. What distinguishes it: every prior coverage attempt spent its signal on queue selection, i.e. on WHEN events fire; none changed WHICH node the run damages, and h3 (two distinct nodes crash and recover) is a precondition for the target class. The fresh stratification says the 2+-recovery stratum is reached by accident (36% of runs) and is not starving (blocked-step share 0.067%), so it is a placement problem, not a budget or frontier problem. The tag has tens of values touched once or twice per run, so unlike the 2.2M-key timeline channel its visit counts stay informative all session instead of saturating in 200-300 runs. Cost: scheduler-core change plus counters plus one bool gate; no exec.rs touch, no dose parameter.",
    "generalityArgument": "The tag reads only simulator-level facts every spec has: restart count, whether undelivered sends are outstanding, how many peers are down. The rule is 'crash the node whose fault context this session has explored least' — it encodes no knowledge of what a crash means to the protocol and names no handler, message, role or timer.",
    "status": "proposed",
    "branch": null,
    "prUrls": [],
    "createdAtIso": "2026-08-30T02:35:00.000Z",
    "firingCounter": null,
    "notes": "Falsifier, per-run factor, screen-stage: depth>=6 events per explore-second not separable from baseline over the sequential protocol. Pre-registered fire criterion (result is uninterpretable, not negative, below it): candidates_ge2 >= 50% of decisions AND diverged_from_uniform >= 10% of decisions AND distinct_tags >= 20 in a 1,080-run session. If candidates_ge2 < 50% the crash subject is structurally near-determined and this closes rather than being retried with a knob."
  },
